# WhenWorks Refactor & Cleanup Specs

This file tracks planned refactor/cleanup work for WhenWorks, following the same `Spec/*.ospec`
convention as `bugfixes.ospec` (see CLAUDE.md). Unlike a bugfix, none of these entries address
broken behavior — the app works correctly today. They bring the existing code in line with
`CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`, which was written after most of the code, so a gap had built up between
the documented standard and what's actually in the files.

The four entries below are staged as independently reviewable milestones, in the order they're
meant to be implemented (CLEANUP-2 depends on CLEANUP-1 being done first for the files it touches;
CLEANUP-3 is written against the file layout CLEANUP-2 produces). No entry changes the database
schema, modifies `Areas/Identity` scaffolding, or adds any new user-facing functionality
(availability voting / chat) — those are all explicitly out of scope, per CLAUDE.md's boundaries.

---

## CLEANUP-1: Mechanical & low-risk convention cleanup (Controllers/Services/Models/Data)

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
Bring the C# code in `Controllers/`, `Services/`, `Models/`, and `Data/` in line with
`CODING_CONVENTIONS.md` on namespace style, constructor style, magic-value centralization, and a
few EF/logic issues — without any structural reorganization (that's CLEANUP-2) or doc-comment
backfill (that's CLEANUP-3).

### Motivation
A full codebase review (see conversation history) found: block-scoped namespaces in files that
have since had substantive edits; classic constructors on several DI-only classes that do no real
work in the constructor; the literal `"ff66c4"` default participant color duplicated
independently in four places; one hardcoded field-length magic number that should reference
`ModelConstants`; two `required` properties that also carry a now-unreachable default value; one
confirmed-dead method; and a couple of EF/logic issues (`MyEventsController.Index` sorting an
already-materialized list in memory instead of in the query; `EventsController` computing the
"does this participant need a rejoin code" rule two different ways instead of reusing its own
`RequiresRejoinCode` helper).

Two judgment calls inform several of the changes below, so they're not silent departures from the
convention text:
- **`required`/`init` applies to controller-constructed display view models, not form-bound
  ones.** `MyEventViewModel`/`EventHomeViewModel` are built directly by controller code
  (`new Foo { ... }`), so the compiler enforces `required` there. `IndexViewModel` and
  `EventSignInViewModel` are constructed by the MVC model binder via reflection, which bypasses
  that compile-time check — `required` on a bound property doesn't actually stop the binder from
  leaving it unset; `[Required]` + `ModelState` (already used correctly on both) is the real
  enforcement. So the shape tightening below only applies `required`/`init` to properties that are
  always set by controller code and never touched by binding.
- **EF navigation properties keep `= null!;`.** The seven occurrences across
  `Event.cs`/`Participant.cs`/`ApplicationUser.cs` look like the "don't add `!` to silence a real
  null case" rule, but they're the standard EF Core idiom: EF always populates them on
  `.Include()`, and app code intentionally constructs these entities by FK id only (e.g.
  `TryCreateNewParticipantAsync`'s `new Participant { EventId = ..., ... }` never sets the `Event`
  nav property). Making them `required` would break that FK-only construction pattern; making them
  nullable would misrepresent the domain. These are explicitly **not** part of this cleanup.

### Proposed Changes

**Namespaces → file-scoped** (`namespace WhenWorksWeb.X;`):
`Controllers/HomeController.cs`, `Services/UniqueCodeGenerator.cs`, `Common/Constants.cs`,
`Models/ApplicationUser.cs`, `Models/Event.cs`, `Models/Participant.cs`, `Models/IdentityConfiguration.cs`.

**Primary constructors** (pure field-assignment constructors → primary constructor syntax):
`Controllers/MyEventsController.cs`, `Services/UniqueCodeGenerator.cs`,
`Areas/Admin/Pages/Users/ManageUsers.cshtml.cs` (`ManageUsersModel`), `Data/Seed/DevelopmentDataSeeder.cs`.
(`EventsController` and `ApplicationDbContext` are already correct as-is and are not touched.)

**Centralize the duplicated default color:** add `ModelConstants.DefaultParticipantColor = "ff66c4"`
to `Common/Constants.cs`; replace the independent `"ff66c4"` literals in `Models/ApplicationUser.cs:32`,
`Models/EventSignInViewModel.cs:42`, `Controllers/EventsController.cs:290`, and
`Data/Seed/DevelopmentDataSeeder.cs:55` with a reference to it.

**Fix the remaining magic number:** `Models/IndexViewModel.cs:15`'s `StringLength(30, ...)` →
`ModelConstants.EventTitleMaxLength`.

**View model shape tightening:**
- `Models/ErrorViewModel.cs` — make `sealed`; `Title`/`ReturnButtonText` → `required ... { get; init; }`;
  `Message`/`ReturnUrl` stay nullable `init` (genuinely optional).
- `ParticipantSelectionViewModel` (in `Models/EventSignInViewModel.cs`) — `DisplayName`/`Color`
  from `required ... { get; set; }` to `required ... { get; init; }`; `IsAssociatedWithCurrentUser`
  from `set` to `init`.
- `Models/EventSignInViewModel.cs` — `EventName` and `ExistingParticipants` (only ever set once,
  by `BuildSignInViewModelAsync`) become `required string EventName { get; init; }` and
  `required IReadOnlyList<ParticipantSelectionViewModel> ExistingParticipants { get; init; }`.
  `DisplayName`, `Color`, `SelectedExistingDisplayName`, `RejoinCode`, `ShowRejoinCodeInput` stay
  mutable `set` (reassigned post-construction by `NormalizeSignInModel`/`ApplySignInViewModelState`).
  Add a one-line comment on the class explaining the mixed shape.
- `Models/IndexViewModel.cs` — leave `{ get; set; }` + `[Required]` as-is (fully bound); just make
  the class `sealed` and apply the magic-number fix above.

**Dead/redundant value cleanup:**
- Remove the now-unreachable default initializers on `EventSettings.Emoji` (`Models/Event.cs:95`)
  and `EventMessage.SentAt` (`Models/Participant.cs:110`) — both are `required`, so the default can
  never be observed through normal construction.
- Remove `Models/IdentityRoleSeeder.cs`'s `AddUserToRoleByEmailAsync` — confirmed unused anywhere
  in the repo.
- `Services/UniqueCodeGenerator.cs` — drop the local `UniqueCodeLength` const alias; reference
  `ModelConstants.UniqueCodeLength` directly in `GenerateCode`.
- `Models/IdentityConfiguration.cs` — change from `public class` with one static method to
  `public static class`, matching `IdentityRoleSeeder`'s pattern.

**EF Core / logic fixes:**
- `Controllers/MyEventsController.cs` `Index` (lines 47-67) — move `.OrderBy(e => e.Title).ThenBy(e => e.Code)`
  into the query before `.ToListAsync()` instead of sorting the materialized list afterward.
- `Controllers/EventsController.cs` `BuildSignInViewModelAsync` (lines 296-298) — replace the
  duplicated inline `showRejoinCodeInput` computation with a call to the existing
  `RequiresRejoinCode(currentParticipant, currentUser?.Id)` helper.
- `Controllers/MyEventsController.cs` — extract the repeated "must be authenticated, else
  `Challenge()`" block (lines 36-41 and 97-101) into one private helper used by both `Index` and `Delete`.
- `Controllers/MyEventsController.cs` `Delete`'s `"participant"` branch — add an explicit
  `await transaction.RollbackAsync(cancellationToken);` before the early `NotFound()` return inside
  the `try`, matching the explicit rollback the `catch` block already does.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `dotnet build` succeeds with no new warnings from `EnableNETAnalyzers`/`EnforceCodeStyleInBuild`.
- `dotnet format` reports no outstanding style violations in the touched files.
- Manual click-through (create event, join event, sign in as new participant, sign in as existing
  participant via rejoin code, event home, My Events list, delete participant, delete event) behaves
  identically to before the change — this is a pure refactor, no behavior change expected anywhere.
- No `.cs` file outside the list above is modified.

### Out of Scope
- The `EventsController` partial-class split (CLEANUP-2).
- Any doc-comment additions (CLEANUP-3).
- Any view/JS changes (CLEANUP-4).
- The `= null!;` EF navigation-property pattern (see Motivation — intentionally not touched).
- Any database schema or migration change.

